        {
          "tool_use_id": "toolu_01UYAkcsyLgsHrAtvUmGqrQ9",
          "tool_name": "write_file",
          "input_json": "{\u0022path\u0022: \u0022pages/single-composition-point.md\u0022, \u0022content\u0022: \u0022---\\ntype: Concept\\ntitle: Single Composition Point for Critical Decisions\\ndescription: Credential-scoping and runtime-path configuration both enforce centralized, auditable handling of security and configuration concerns.\\ntags:\\n  - source-type/synthesis\\n  - concept/Single-Composition-Point\\n  - tech/dotnet\\nconfidence: high\\nconfidence_reason: \\\u0022Both [[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] independently articulate the same architectural principle: critical decisions are made once at a central point, not scattered or repeated throughout the codebase.\\\u0022\\nreview_date: 2025-07-14\\n---\\n\\n## The Connection\\n\\n[[credential-scoping]] and [[runtime-paths]] are two expressions of the same architectural principle: **critical security and configuration decisions should be composed once, in a single place, rather than repeated or discovered ad-hoc throughout the codebase.**\\n\\n[[runtime-paths]] establishes the mechanism: a single path resolver at startup reads configuration (command line, environment, config file, or code defaults), validates it, and provides the authoritative resolved locations to the rest of the Hub. No other part of the codebase directly resolves file paths; all downstream code consumes the pre-resolved set. This achieves **centrality** (one resolver, one decision) and **auditability** (the source and value of every location is logged at startup).\\n\\n[[credential-scoping]] applies this same principle to secrets: the Anthropic API key is not scattered across process environments or discovered on demand. Instead, a startup-time secrets loader reads the key from a specific location (determined by the path resolver) and injects it into only the child-process environment of the agents that need it. The Hub\u0027s own process never holds it.\\n\\nTogether, they mean that whether you are asking \\\u0022where does this resource live?\\\u0022 (runtime paths) or \\\u0022who can see this secret?\\\u0022 (credential scoping), the answer is determined once, transparently, at a single startup composition point \u2014 not buried in runtime logic or scattered across multiple code paths.\\n\\nThis pattern is valuable because it makes security and configuration decisions visible and auditable at one moment in time, rather than implicit in the runtime behavior of many components. Adding a new runtime location or credential consumer means extending one resolver or one scoping decision, not threading logic through the codebase.\\n\u0022}"
        }
      ],
      "assistant_text": "Excellent \u2014 now I can see the connection. Let me draft the answer first, then decide if this warrants a synthesis article.\n\n## Answer\n\nThe two decisions are complementary **scoping principles** that operate at different levels to enforce the Hub\u0027s security and configuration architecture:\n\n**Credential-scoping** [[credential-scoping]] controls *who* can access secrets: the Anthropic API key is scoped to the child-process environment of the Ingest and Query agents alone, never exposed in the Hub\u0027s own process memory. This prevents a compromised Hub from ever seeing the credential.\n\n**Runtime-path configuration** [[runtime-paths]] controls *where* critical resources live: all file locations are resolved from a single configuration point at startup, so the source of truth for where credentials, data, and configuration actually reside is centralized and auditable.\n\nTogether, they work like this: the secrets loader uses the path configuration to locate the git-ignored \u0060.env\u0060 file containing the API key (the *where*), and then injects it only into the spawned agent\u0027s environment (the *who*). Neither the credential\u0027s location nor the agent\u0027s access to it is scattered across the codebase \u2014 both are unified decisions made at startup.\n\nThis is why the articles are in the same conceptual family: **credential-scoping and runtime-path configuration are both expressions of the principle that critical security and configuration decisions should be made once, in a single place, rather than repeatedly or ad-hoc throughout the code.** Runtime-path configuration provides the mechanism; credential-scoping applies that mechanism specifically to secrets.\n\n---\n\n## Synthesis Judgment\n\nThis connection is genuine and new: neither article explicitly states their relationship to each other, but reading both together reveals they are jointly enforcing a shared architectural principle \u2014 centralization and auditability of critical decisions. This is worth preserving as a concept article.\n\nLet me save this as a synthesis article:",
